WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:09.036 --> 00:11.256 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:17.126 --> 00:21.126 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:22.186 --> 00:25.326 align:middle
Today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:25.356 --> 00:28.896 align:middle
to maintain the target range
for the federal funds rate

00:29.456 --> 00:31.936 align:middle
at 1/4 to 1/2 percent.

00:33.146 --> 00:36.716 align:middle
Our decision to keep this
accommodative policy stance

00:37.146 --> 00:40.616 align:middle
reflects both our assessment
of the economic outlook

00:41.056 --> 00:43.746 align:middle
and the risks associated
with that outlook.

00:44.536 --> 00:46.826 align:middle
The Committee's baseline
expectations

00:46.826 --> 00:49.776 align:middle
for economic activity,
the labor market,

00:49.776 --> 00:53.576 align:middle
and inflation have not
changed much since December:

00:54.486 --> 00:56.706 align:middle
With appropriate
monetary policy,

00:57.036 --> 01:00.176 align:middle
we continue to expect
moderate economic growth,

01:00.676 --> 01:03.926 align:middle
further labor market
improvement, and a return

01:03.926 --> 01:08.166 align:middle
of inflation to our 2 percent
objective in two to three years.

01:09.076 --> 01:11.136 align:middle
However, global economic

01:11.136 --> 01:14.466 align:middle
and financial developments
continue to pose risks.

01:15.226 --> 01:19.296 align:middle
Against this backdrop, the
Committee judged it prudent

01:19.606 --> 01:23.026 align:middle
to maintain the current policy
stance at today's meeting.

01:23.396 --> 01:27.266 align:middle
I will come back to our
policy decision momentarily,

01:27.676 --> 01:31.026 align:middle
but first let me review
recent economic developments

01:31.026 --> 01:31.776 align:middle
and the outlook.

01:33.036 --> 01:35.826 align:middle
The labor market
continues to strengthen.

01:36.576 --> 01:38.586 align:middle
Over the most recent
three months,

01:38.886 --> 01:43.276 align:middle
job gains averaged
nearly 230,000 per month,

01:44.106 --> 01:47.666 align:middle
similar to the pace
experienced over the past year.

01:48.596 --> 01:52.376 align:middle
The unemployment rate was 4.9
percent in the first two months

01:52.376 --> 01:55.376 align:middle
of the year, about in
line with the median

01:55.376 --> 02:00.286 align:middle
of FOMC participants' estimates
of its longer-run normal level.

02:01.326 --> 02:03.196 align:middle
A broader measure
of unemployment

02:03.196 --> 02:07.656 align:middle
that includes individuals who
want and are available to work

02:08.206 --> 02:11.656 align:middle
but have not actively
searched recently and people

02:11.656 --> 02:13.076 align:middle
who are working part time

02:13.076 --> 02:17.046 align:middle
but would rather work full
time has continued to improve.

02:17.856 --> 02:21.786 align:middle
Of note, the labor force
participation rate has turned

02:21.786 --> 02:25.696 align:middle
up noticeably since the fall,
with more people working

02:25.696 --> 02:29.246 align:middle
or actively looking for
work as the prospects

02:29.246 --> 02:31.286 align:middle
for finding jobs have improved.

02:32.136 --> 02:34.106 align:middle
But there is still
room for improvement:

02:34.886 --> 02:38.676 align:middle
Involuntary part-time employment
remains somewhat elevated,

02:39.296 --> 02:42.396 align:middle
and wage growth has yet to
show a sustained pickup.

02:42.916 --> 02:46.126 align:middle
The improvement in
employment conditions

02:46.126 --> 02:50.646 align:middle
so far this year has occurred
as economic growth appears

02:50.646 --> 02:53.646 align:middle
to have picked up from
the modest pace seen

02:53.646 --> 02:55.476 align:middle
in the fourth quarter
of last year.

02:56.646 --> 02:59.856 align:middle
Household spending is
expanding at a moderate rate,

03:00.116 --> 03:03.886 align:middle
supported by continued
job gains and increases

03:03.886 --> 03:05.926 align:middle
in inflation-adjusted incomes.

03:06.846 --> 03:10.106 align:middle
In contrast, business
investment has been weak,

03:10.646 --> 03:14.286 align:middle
in part reflecting further
reductions in oil drilling

03:14.706 --> 03:16.866 align:middle
as a result of low oil prices.

03:17.976 --> 03:22.626 align:middle
Net exports also remain
soft as a consequence

03:22.896 --> 03:24.596 align:middle
of subdued foreign growth

03:25.106 --> 03:27.636 align:middle
and the earlier appreciation
of the dollar.

03:28.736 --> 03:31.286 align:middle
Looking ahead, the
Committee expects that,

03:31.286 --> 03:35.186 align:middle
with gradual adjustments in
the stance of monetary policy,

03:35.796 --> 03:39.666 align:middle
economic activity will continue
to expand at a moderate pace

03:40.186 --> 03:43.476 align:middle
and labor market indicators
will continue to strengthen.

03:44.956 --> 03:48.736 align:middle
Ongoing economic growth and
additional strengthening

03:48.736 --> 03:53.056 align:middle
in labor market conditions are
important factors underpinning

03:53.056 --> 03:54.276 align:middle
the inflation outlook.

03:55.346 --> 03:57.756 align:middle
Overall consumer
price inflation--

03:58.226 --> 04:00.026 align:middle
as measured by the price index

04:00.056 --> 04:02.456 align:middle
for personal consumption
expenditures--

04:02.996 --> 04:08.476 align:middle
stepped up to 1-1/4 percent over
the 12 months ending in January,

04:09.076 --> 04:13.106 align:middle
as the sharp decline in
energy prices around the end

04:13.106 --> 04:17.666 align:middle
of 2014 dropped out of the
year-over-year figures.

04:18.606 --> 04:22.866 align:middle
Core inflation, which excludes
energy and food prices,

04:23.406 --> 04:27.146 align:middle
has also picked up, although
it remains to be seen

04:27.146 --> 04:29.406 align:middle
if this firming will
be sustained.

04:30.336 --> 04:34.236 align:middle
In particular, the earlier
declines in energy prices

04:34.236 --> 04:38.516 align:middle
and appreciation of the dollar
could well continue to weigh

04:38.516 --> 04:40.496 align:middle
on overall consumer prices.

04:41.516 --> 04:44.396 align:middle
But once these transitory
influences fade

04:44.796 --> 04:47.256 align:middle
and as the labor market
strengthens further,

04:47.896 --> 04:51.326 align:middle
the Committee expects
inflation to rise to 2 percent

04:51.596 --> 04:53.436 align:middle
over the next two
to three years.

04:54.346 --> 04:57.766 align:middle
The Committee's inflation
outlook rests importantly

04:57.766 --> 05:02.306 align:middle
on its judgment that longer-run
inflation expectations remain

05:02.306 --> 05:03.676 align:middle
reasonably well anchored.

05:04.856 --> 05:06.076 align:middle
However, the stability

05:06.076 --> 05:09.456 align:middle
of longer-run inflation
expectations cannot be taken

05:09.456 --> 05:10.266 align:middle
for granted.

05:11.126 --> 05:12.546 align:middle
Survey-based measures

05:12.546 --> 05:15.766 align:middle
of longer-run inflation
expectations are little changed,

05:15.766 --> 05:17.946 align:middle
on balance, in recent months,

05:18.596 --> 05:21.486 align:middle
although some remain near
historically low levels.

05:22.776 --> 05:23.986 align:middle
Market-based measures

05:23.986 --> 05:27.346 align:middle
of inflation compensation
also remain low.

05:28.376 --> 05:31.526 align:middle
Movements in these
indicators reflect many factors

05:32.026 --> 05:36.036 align:middle
and therefore may not provide
an accurate reading on changes

05:36.466 --> 05:39.766 align:middle
in the inflation expectations
that are most relevant

05:40.106 --> 05:41.756 align:middle
for wage and price setting.

05:42.756 --> 05:47.016 align:middle
Nonetheless, our statement
continues to emphasize that,

05:47.016 --> 05:49.766 align:middle
in considering future
policy decisions,

05:50.246 --> 05:52.556 align:middle
we will carefully monitor actual

05:52.556 --> 05:55.946 align:middle
and expected progress
toward our inflation goal.

05:57.316 --> 06:00.116 align:middle
This general assessment of
the outlook is reflected

06:00.116 --> 06:03.236 align:middle
in the individual economic
projections submitted

06:03.296 --> 06:06.646 align:middle
for this meeting by
FOMC participants.

06:07.546 --> 06:12.016 align:middle
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

06:12.016 --> 06:15.336 align:middle
on his or own-- his
or her own view

06:15.686 --> 06:19.386 align:middle
of appropriate monetary
policy, which, in turn,

06:19.386 --> 06:23.136 align:middle
depends on each person's
assessment of the multitude

06:23.136 --> 06:25.046 align:middle
of factors that shape
the outlook.

06:25.826 --> 06:28.296 align:middle
Participants' projections
for growth

06:28.296 --> 06:32.286 align:middle
of inflation-adjusted
gross domestic product

06:32.286 --> 06:36.036 align:middle
or GDP are just a touch lower
than the projections made

06:36.296 --> 06:39.606 align:middle
in conjunction with the
December FOMC meeting.

06:40.446 --> 06:42.726 align:middle
The median growth
projection edges

06:42.726 --> 06:48.626 align:middle
down from 2.2 percent this
year to 2 percent in 2018,

06:49.086 --> 06:51.916 align:middle
in line with its
estimated longer-run rate.

06:53.186 --> 06:57.726 align:middle
The median projection for the
unemployment rate falls from 4.7

06:57.726 --> 07:02.886 align:middle
at the end of this year to 4.5
percent at the end of 2018,

07:03.506 --> 07:06.096 align:middle
somewhat below the
median assessment

07:06.096 --> 07:08.676 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

07:09.526 --> 07:10.856 align:middle
The median path

07:10.856 --> 07:14.256 align:middle
of the unemployment rate is a
little lower than in December,

07:14.966 --> 07:18.896 align:middle
in part reflecting a
slightly lower median estimate

07:18.976 --> 07:21.926 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

07:22.646 --> 07:25.826 align:middle
Finally, with the
transitory factors holding

07:25.826 --> 07:28.816 align:middle
down inflation expected to abate

07:29.316 --> 07:31.816 align:middle
and labor market
conditions anticipated

07:31.816 --> 07:33.186 align:middle
to strengthen further,

07:33.396 --> 07:38.496 align:middle
the median inflation projection
rises from 1.2 percent this year

07:39.076 --> 07:43.796 align:middle
to 1.9 percent next year
and 2 percent in 2018.

07:44.026 --> 07:47.626 align:middle
The median inflation projection
for this year is a little lower

07:47.626 --> 07:51.516 align:middle
than in December, but thereafter
the median projections

07:51.516 --> 07:52.416 align:middle
are unchanged.

07:53.726 --> 07:55.416 align:middle
Since the turn of the year,

07:55.416 --> 08:00.186 align:middle
concerns about global
economic prospects have led

08:00.186 --> 08:03.056 align:middle
to increased financial
market volatility

08:03.696 --> 08:07.476 align:middle
and somewhat tighter financial
conditions in the United States,

08:08.286 --> 08:11.446 align:middle
although financial conditions
have improved notably

08:11.446 --> 08:12.536 align:middle
more recently.

08:13.656 --> 08:18.136 align:middle
In addition, economic growth
abroad appears to be running

08:18.136 --> 08:22.186 align:middle
at a somewhat softer pace
than previously expected.

08:23.116 --> 08:26.196 align:middle
These unanticipated
developments, however,

08:26.706 --> 08:30.676 align:middle
have not resulted
in material changes

08:31.046 --> 08:33.306 align:middle
to the Committee's
baseline outlook.

08:34.456 --> 08:39.076 align:middle
One reason for this is that
market expectations for the path

08:39.076 --> 08:42.026 align:middle
of policy interest
rates have moved down,

08:42.846 --> 08:44.706 align:middle
and the accompanying decline

08:44.706 --> 08:49.196 align:middle
in longer-term interest rates
should help cushion any possible

08:49.246 --> 08:52.886 align:middle
adverse effects on
domestic economic activity.

08:54.026 --> 08:57.036 align:middle
Indeed, while stock
prices have fallen slightly

08:57.036 --> 09:00.156 align:middle
since the December
meeting and spreads

09:00.156 --> 09:03.436 align:middle
of investment-grade corporate
bond yields over those

09:03.436 --> 09:07.666 align:middle
on comparable-maturity
Treasury securities have risen,

09:08.456 --> 09:11.756 align:middle
mortgage rates and
corporate borrowing costs have

09:11.786 --> 09:12.576 align:middle
moved lower.

09:13.536 --> 09:15.746 align:middle
Of course, the Committee
will continue

09:15.746 --> 09:18.236 align:middle
to monitor these
developments closely

09:18.706 --> 09:22.596 align:middle
and will adjust the stance
of monetary policy as needed

09:22.596 --> 09:25.376 align:middle
to foster our goals
of maximum employment

09:25.726 --> 09:27.286 align:middle
and 2 percent inflation.

09:28.386 --> 09:31.486 align:middle
Returning to monetary
policy, as I noted earlier,

09:31.906 --> 09:34.916 align:middle
the Committee decided to
maintain its target range

09:34.916 --> 09:36.236 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

09:37.326 --> 09:41.276 align:middle
This decision partly
reflects the implications

09:41.276 --> 09:45.256 align:middle
for the U.S. economy
of the global economic

09:45.256 --> 09:48.036 align:middle
and financial developments
I just mentioned.

09:48.806 --> 09:51.896 align:middle
In addition, proceeding
cautiously

09:51.936 --> 09:56.656 align:middle
in removing policy accommodation
at this time will allow us

09:56.656 --> 09:59.656 align:middle
to verify that the labor
market is continuing

09:59.686 --> 10:03.016 align:middle
to strengthen despite
the risks from abroad.

10:03.826 --> 10:06.756 align:middle
Such caution is appropriate
given

10:06.756 --> 10:09.836 align:middle
that short-term interest
rates are still near zero,

10:10.476 --> 10:14.496 align:middle
which means that monetary policy
has greater scope to respond

10:14.936 --> 10:18.636 align:middle
to upside than to downside
changes in the outlook.

10:19.796 --> 10:21.896 align:middle
As we indicated in
our statement,

10:22.256 --> 10:25.986 align:middle
"the Committee expects that
economic conditions will evolve

10:26.026 --> 10:30.146 align:middle
in a manner that will
warrant only gradual increases

10:30.146 --> 10:33.996 align:middle
in the federal funds rate; the
federal funds rate is likely

10:34.046 --> 10:38.076 align:middle
to remain, for some time,
below levels that are expected

10:38.076 --> 10:39.956 align:middle
to prevail in the longer run."

10:40.716 --> 10:43.886 align:middle
This expectation is
consistent with the view

10:43.886 --> 10:46.516 align:middle
that the neutral nominal
federal funds rate--

10:47.296 --> 10:49.676 align:middle
defined as the value of
the federal funds rate

10:49.676 --> 10:53.746 align:middle
that would be neither
expansionary nor contractionary

10:54.076 --> 10:56.826 align:middle
if the economy was
operating near potential--

10:57.586 --> 11:01.966 align:middle
is currently low by historical
standards and is likely

11:01.996 --> 11:04.496 align:middle
to rise only gradually
over time.

11:05.226 --> 11:08.186 align:middle
The low level of the neutral
federal funds rate may be

11:08.186 --> 11:10.926 align:middle
partially attributable
to a range

11:10.926 --> 11:15.236 align:middle
of persistent economic headwinds
that weigh on aggregate demand,

11:15.726 --> 11:19.396 align:middle
including developments
abroad, a subdued pace

11:19.396 --> 11:23.016 align:middle
of household formation, and
meager productivity growth.

11:24.186 --> 11:26.896 align:middle
There is considerable
uncertainty regarding the

11:26.896 --> 11:29.786 align:middle
evolution of the neutral
funds rate over time.

11:30.306 --> 11:34.286 align:middle
However, if these headwinds
abate, as we expect,

11:34.706 --> 11:37.766 align:middle
the neutral federal funds rate
should gradually move higher

11:37.766 --> 11:38.326 align:middle
as well.

11:39.446 --> 11:41.926 align:middle
This view is implicitly
reflected

11:41.926 --> 11:46.366 align:middle
in participants' projections
of appropriate monetary policy.

11:47.036 --> 11:51.416 align:middle
The median projection for the
federal funds rate rises only

11:51.416 --> 11:55.736 align:middle
gradually to 0.9
percent late this year

11:56.166 --> 11:58.516 align:middle
and 1.9 percent next year.

11:59.516 --> 12:03.096 align:middle
As the factors restraining
economic growth are projected

12:03.156 --> 12:08.536 align:middle
to fade further over time, the
median rate rises to 3 percent

12:08.536 --> 12:13.406 align:middle
by the end of 2018, close to
its longer-run normal level.

12:14.576 --> 12:17.386 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in December,

12:17.826 --> 12:22.856 align:middle
the median path is about 1/2
percentage point lower this year

12:22.856 --> 12:28.226 align:middle
and next; the median longer-run
normal federal funds rate has

12:28.266 --> 12:29.966 align:middle
been revised down as well.

12:31.126 --> 12:35.226 align:middle
In other words, most Committee
participants now expect

12:35.626 --> 12:40.526 align:middle
that achieving economic outcomes
similar to those anticipated

12:40.526 --> 12:45.006 align:middle
in December will likely
require a somewhat lower path

12:45.006 --> 12:49.266 align:middle
for policy interest rates
than foreseen at that time.

12:50.276 --> 12:52.436 align:middle
I would like to underscore,
however,

12:53.006 --> 12:55.376 align:middle
that the participants'
projections

12:55.376 --> 12:59.176 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,
including the median path,

12:59.876 --> 13:02.876 align:middle
are not a "plan"
for future policy.

13:03.906 --> 13:06.736 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

13:07.606 --> 13:11.086 align:middle
These forecasts represent
participants' individual

13:11.086 --> 13:15.536 align:middle
assessments of what appropriate
policy would be given each

13:15.536 --> 13:20.556 align:middle
person's own current projections
of the most likely outcomes

13:20.846 --> 13:25.376 align:middle
for economic growth, employment,
inflation, and other factors.

13:26.096 --> 13:29.186 align:middle
However, considerable
uncertainty attaches

13:29.186 --> 13:33.256 align:middle
to each participant's
forecasts of economic outcomes.

13:34.106 --> 13:35.876 align:middle
Hence, their assessments

13:35.876 --> 13:40.296 align:middle
of appropriate policy are
also uncertain and will change

13:40.296 --> 13:43.416 align:middle
in response to adjustments
to the economic outlook

13:43.896 --> 13:48.966 align:middle
and associated risks, as was the
case between December and now.

13:50.156 --> 13:51.806 align:middle
Also, it is important to note

13:51.806 --> 13:53.816 align:middle
that the Committee
makes its decisions

13:54.026 --> 13:58.696 align:middle
on a meeting-by-meeting basis
and does not and need not decide

13:58.696 --> 14:02.006 align:middle
on a likely future path
for the federal funds rate.

14:02.926 --> 14:06.656 align:middle
Indeed, the future path of
policy is necessarily uncertain

14:07.366 --> 14:11.416 align:middle
because the economy will surely
evolve in unexpected ways.

14:12.366 --> 14:15.176 align:middle
As we note in our
statement, "the actual path

14:15.176 --> 14:19.206 align:middle
of the federal funds rate will
depend on the economic outlook

14:19.206 --> 14:21.016 align:middle
as informed by incoming data."

14:22.296 --> 14:25.956 align:middle
Finally, the Committee
will continue its policy

14:26.416 --> 14:30.436 align:middle
of reinvesting proceeds from
maturing Treasury securities

14:31.006 --> 14:33.436 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency debt

14:33.436 --> 14:35.276 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities.

14:36.056 --> 14:38.356 align:middle
As highlighted in
our policy statement,

14:38.886 --> 14:43.206 align:middle
we anticipate continuing this
policy "until normalization

14:43.206 --> 14:46.156 align:middle
of the level of the federal
funds rate is well under way."

14:47.156 --> 14:49.046 align:middle
Maintaining our sizable holdings

14:49.046 --> 14:53.546 align:middle
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

14:53.546 --> 14:57.026 align:middle
financial conditions and
should reduce the risk

14:57.026 --> 15:00.576 align:middle
that we might have to lower
the federal funds rate to zero

15:00.846 --> 15:04.626 align:middle
in the event of a future
large adverse shock.

15:05.316 --> 15:08.466 align:middle
Thank you, and I'll be happy
to take your questions.

15:09.286 --> 15:10.196 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

15:13.276 --> 15:14.576 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

15:14.986 --> 15:18.916 align:middle
Madam Chair, as you
know, inflation has gone

15:18.916 --> 15:19.916 align:middle
up the last two months.

15:20.306 --> 15:24.376 align:middle
We had another strong jobs
report, the tracking forecasts

15:24.376 --> 15:26.136 align:middle
for GDP have returned
to 2 percent,

15:26.626 --> 15:29.616 align:middle
and yet the Fed stands pat
while it's in a process

15:29.616 --> 15:31.966 align:middle
of what it said it
launched in December was a

15:31.966 --> 15:33.356 align:middle
"process of normalization."

15:33.356 --> 15:34.896 align:middle
So I have two questions
about this:

15:34.896 --> 15:37.056 align:middle
Does the Fed have a credibility
problem, in the sense

15:37.056 --> 15:38.886 align:middle
that it says it will do some--

15:38.886 --> 15:40.016 align:middle
one thing under certain
conditions

15:40.016 --> 15:41.626 align:middle
but doesn't end up doing it?

15:41.626 --> 15:42.726 align:middle
And then, frankly,

15:42.966 --> 15:45.476 align:middle
if the current conditions are
not sufficient for the Fed

15:45.476 --> 15:47.806 align:middle
to raise rates, well, what would
those conditions ever look like?

15:48.886 --> 15:50.036 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

15:50.036 --> 15:53.516 align:middle
Well, let me start-- let
me start with the question

15:53.516 --> 15:59.226 align:middle
of the Fed's credibility, and
you used the word "promises"

15:59.226 --> 16:00.806 align:middle
in connection with that.

16:01.156 --> 16:04.866 align:middle
And, as I tried to emphasize
in my opening statement,

16:05.456 --> 16:10.996 align:middle
the paths that the
participants project

16:11.396 --> 16:14.266 align:middle
for the federal funds rate

16:14.266 --> 16:20.576 align:middle
and how it will evolve are not
a preset plan or commitment

16:20.576 --> 16:22.586 align:middle
or promise of the Committee.

16:22.896 --> 16:27.806 align:middle
Indeed, they are not even-- the
median should not be interpreted

16:28.016 --> 16:34.366 align:middle
as a Committee-endorsed
forecast, and there's a lot

16:34.366 --> 16:38.426 align:middle
of uncertainty around each
participant's projection.

16:38.926 --> 16:41.536 align:middle
And they will evolve--

16:41.716 --> 16:49.516 align:middle
those assessments of appropriate
policy are completely contingent

16:49.736 --> 16:54.066 align:middle
on each participant's
forecast of the economy

16:54.066 --> 16:58.236 align:middle
and how economic
events will unfold.

16:58.716 --> 17:01.506 align:middle
And they are, of
course, uncertain,

17:01.796 --> 17:06.436 align:middle
and you should fully
expect that forecasts

17:06.436 --> 17:09.716 align:middle
for the appropriate path
of policy on the part

17:09.716 --> 17:15.626 align:middle
of all participants will evolve
over time as shocks, positive

17:15.626 --> 17:20.336 align:middle
or negative, hit the economy
that alter those forecasts.

17:20.616 --> 17:25.246 align:middle
So you have seen
a shift this time

17:25.466 --> 17:27.786 align:middle
in most participants'
assessments

17:27.786 --> 17:30.896 align:middle
of the appropriate
path for policy, and,

17:30.896 --> 17:32.736 align:middle
as I tried to indicate, I think

17:32.736 --> 17:38.356 align:middle
that largely reflects a
somewhat slower projected path

17:38.416 --> 17:41.086 align:middle
for global growth-- for growth

17:41.406 --> 17:44.936 align:middle
in the global economy
outside the United States--

17:45.626 --> 17:49.866 align:middle
and for some tightening in
credit conditions in the form

17:49.866 --> 17:52.406 align:middle
of an increase in spreads.

17:52.836 --> 17:59.186 align:middle
And those changes in financial
conditions and in the path

17:59.186 --> 18:04.476 align:middle
of the global economy
have induced changes

18:04.546 --> 18:07.476 align:middle
in the assessment of
individual participants

18:07.766 --> 18:11.556 align:middle
in what path is appropriate
to achieve our objectives.

18:11.806 --> 18:14.566 align:middle
So that's what you see.

18:14.566 --> 18:15.646 align:middle
That's what you see.

18:15.646 --> 18:20.856 align:middle
Now, I guess you asked me
also what would we need to see

18:21.176 --> 18:26.006 align:middle
to continue raising rates, and
I think it's worth pointing

18:26.006 --> 18:31.776 align:middle
out here that the Committee--
most participants do continue

18:32.136 --> 18:36.446 align:middle
to envision that if
economic developments unfold

18:36.446 --> 18:39.386 align:middle
as they expect, that
further increases

18:39.386 --> 18:43.866 align:middle
in the federal funds rate will
prove appropriate over time.

18:43.996 --> 18:47.356 align:middle
Most participants
anticipate that,

18:47.566 --> 18:50.676 align:middle
and that the pace
will be gradual.

18:50.676 --> 18:55.696 align:middle
As I emphasized, most
empirical work attempting

18:55.746 --> 19:01.876 align:middle
to assess what the equilibrium
level of the fed funds rate is,

19:01.876 --> 19:06.146 align:middle
a level that would be neither
expansionary nor contractionary

19:06.466 --> 19:09.796 align:middle
is-- those assessments are
quite low at this time.

19:10.076 --> 19:13.796 align:middle
So there is accommodation
in the stance of policy,

19:14.206 --> 19:19.836 align:middle
and we do expect, over time,
that neutral rate to move up,

19:20.256 --> 19:24.126 align:middle
but, you know, we're
not positive what rate--

19:24.126 --> 19:27.646 align:middle
what the pace of change
of that will be over time.

19:27.986 --> 19:31.896 align:middle
But, given that the
economy is now close

19:31.896 --> 19:35.946 align:middle
to our maximum employment
objective, you know,

19:36.126 --> 19:38.316 align:middle
hopefully inflation
is moving up--

19:38.316 --> 19:41.706 align:middle
I mentioned, as you
mentioned, recent readings

19:41.706 --> 19:45.486 align:middle
on inflation have moved up.

19:45.526 --> 19:48.166 align:middle
There may be some-- you
know, I want to warn

19:48.166 --> 19:50.586 align:middle
that there may be some
transitory factors

19:50.826 --> 19:53.816 align:middle
that are influencing that.

19:54.076 --> 19:58.226 align:middle
But, certainly, our projections
are for a gradual increase

19:58.226 --> 20:00.656 align:middle
in inflation, and
the Committee--

20:00.776 --> 20:05.076 align:middle
at least, most participants
continue to expect

20:05.116 --> 20:07.266 align:middle
that if we follow
along this course,

20:07.566 --> 20:09.206 align:middle
that some further adjustments

20:09.206 --> 20:12.356 align:middle
in the federal funds rate will
be appropriate but gradual.

20:13.516 --> 20:14.316 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

20:14.456 --> 20:17.606 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

20:17.606 --> 20:19.246 align:middle
Can I just follow up on
this inflation point?

20:19.246 --> 20:20.896 align:middle
Because the numbers
have been ticking up,

20:20.896 --> 20:24.536 align:middle
as you said-- somewhat,
at least.

20:24.536 --> 20:26.536 align:middle
And we're also, as you
said, also at a point

20:26.536 --> 20:29.606 align:middle
where we have quite
close to full employment.

20:29.606 --> 20:31.956 align:middle
Is there a risk that we're
heading for an overshoot

20:32.076 --> 20:34.646 align:middle
in inflation, and is there,

20:34.646 --> 20:37.326 align:middle
given the greater symmetry
the Fed has been flagging up,

20:37.326 --> 20:40.196 align:middle
in terms of its inflation
target, a greater tolerance

20:40.196 --> 20:42.756 align:middle
for a modest overshoot,
especially given the long period

20:42.756 --> 20:44.136 align:middle
of undershoots that
we've been through?

20:44.456 --> 20:45.486 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

20:45.676 --> 20:51.356 align:middle
So I want to make clear that
our inflation objective is 2

20:51.416 --> 20:56.056 align:middle
percent, and we are projecting
a move back to 2 percent.

20:56.056 --> 21:00.006 align:middle
And we are not trying
to engineer an overshoot

21:00.006 --> 21:07.756 align:middle
of inflation, not to
compensate for past undershoots,

21:07.756 --> 21:09.806 align:middle
so 2 percent is our objective.

21:10.186 --> 21:15.376 align:middle
But it is a symmetric objective,
and we certainly don't seek

21:15.436 --> 21:17.436 align:middle
to overshoot our objective.

21:17.826 --> 21:22.696 align:middle
But some undershoots
and overshoots are part

21:22.926 --> 21:28.256 align:middle
of how the economy
operates, and our tolerance

21:28.256 --> 21:31.536 align:middle
for those is symmetric
with respect

21:31.596 --> 21:33.666 align:middle
to under- and overshoots.

21:34.136 --> 21:37.886 align:middle
We did take note in the
statement of the fact

21:37.956 --> 21:41.976 align:middle
that inflation has picked
up in recent months.

21:42.236 --> 21:46.016 align:middle
I see some of that
as having to do

21:46.016 --> 21:52.146 align:middle
with unusually high inflation
readings in categories that tend

21:52.146 --> 21:56.796 align:middle
to be quite volatile without
very much significance

21:56.796 --> 21:59.396 align:middle
for inflation over time.

21:59.896 --> 22:05.756 align:middle
So I'm wary and haven't
yet concluded

22:05.796 --> 22:08.906 align:middle
that we have seen any
significant uptick

22:09.456 --> 22:13.856 align:middle
that will be lasting in, for
example, in core inflation.

22:14.276 --> 22:19.596 align:middle
But we note-- the Committee
notes, as it did in December,

22:19.796 --> 22:22.946 align:middle
that we continue to
monitor development trends

22:23.396 --> 22:28.336 align:middle
and developments closely, and
that would include both the fact

22:28.336 --> 22:32.876 align:middle
that recent inflation readings
have been on the high side, and,

22:32.876 --> 22:38.926 align:middle
as I mentioned, on the other
side, that readings on measures

22:38.926 --> 22:40.756 align:middle
of inflation compensation

22:40.966 --> 22:43.956 align:middle
and some survey measures
have been on the low side.

22:43.956 --> 22:47.536 align:middle
So, in that sense,
there are risks

22:47.536 --> 22:51.856 align:middle
around the inflation forecast
in both-- in both directions.

22:53.316 --> 22:55.436 align:middle
LINDSAY DUNSMUIR.

22:56.286 --> 22:57.986 align:middle
Hi, Lindsay Dunsmuir
with Reuters.

22:58.786 --> 23:01.356 align:middle
Your statement did note
that, as you're saying,

23:01.356 --> 23:04.266 align:middle
that inflation has picked
up in recent months,

23:04.266 --> 23:05.996 align:middle
and you do still
see it going back

23:05.996 --> 23:07.596 align:middle
to 2 percent over
the medium term.

23:08.146 --> 23:12.446 align:middle
Yet policymakers have
downgraded GDP growth forecasts

23:12.446 --> 23:14.906 align:middle
for this year and one of
the inflation measures.

23:15.566 --> 23:19.326 align:middle
So that to me would indicate a
weakening economic environment,

23:19.326 --> 23:21.916 align:middle
and I'm wondering how,
in that environment,

23:22.236 --> 23:25.106 align:middle
you justify the possibility
of two rate hikes this year.

23:25.396 --> 23:25.976 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

23:26.326 --> 23:30.956 align:middle
So there has been a slight
downgrading of the assessment

23:31.136 --> 23:33.526 align:middle
of economic growth
for this year.

23:33.836 --> 23:38.996 align:middle
But, nevertheless, growth
is expected to run in--

23:39.036 --> 23:41.216 align:middle
somewhat in excess of potential,

23:41.556 --> 23:46.476 align:middle
so that the labor market is
expected to continue to tighten,

23:46.866 --> 23:54.136 align:middle
and by the end of the year,
even edge below levels that--

23:54.306 --> 23:59.256 align:middle
of the unemployment rate--
that are estimated to be longer

23:59.256 --> 24:06.746 align:middle
in the normal run, and
inflation is expected

24:06.746 --> 24:10.986 align:middle
to gradually move back
to 2 percent over time.

24:10.986 --> 24:15.166 align:middle
We still have, weighing on
inflation, the influence

24:15.166 --> 24:20.176 align:middle
of earlier declines in energy
prices and a prolonged effect

24:20.176 --> 24:22.426 align:middle
from the appreciation
of the dollar,

24:22.876 --> 24:28.076 align:middle
but we do expect those
transitory influences to fade.

24:28.406 --> 24:30.906 align:middle
And with a continuing--

24:31.616 --> 24:34.686 align:middle
continuing improvement
in the labor market,

24:35.006 --> 24:38.426 align:middle
I think we'll see upward
pressure on inflation.

24:38.866 --> 24:40.636 align:middle
And, in that context,

24:40.916 --> 24:44.016 align:middle
the Committee sees
it appropriate to--

24:45.146 --> 24:49.436 align:middle
if things unfold in that way,
to have some further increases

24:49.436 --> 24:51.866 align:middle
in the federal funds rate.

24:51.866 --> 24:53.506 align:middle
It remains accommodative.

24:53.846 --> 24:59.126 align:middle
As I indicated in December-- the
Committee indicated in December,

24:59.126 --> 25:03.216 align:middle
we want inflation to
go back to 2 percent.

25:03.646 --> 25:06.916 align:middle
But we also want
to be careful not

25:06.966 --> 25:09.846 align:middle
to see some significant
overshoots,

25:09.846 --> 25:14.036 align:middle
so that we would get behind the
curve and potentially be faced

25:14.036 --> 25:19.446 align:middle
with a need to tighten in a very
rapid fashion later in a way

25:19.446 --> 25:22.176 align:middle
that could undermine
the sustainability

25:22.176 --> 25:24.876 align:middle
of the employment
gains we have had.

25:24.996 --> 25:29.696 align:middle
But we do see some continued
tightening in monetary policy

25:30.036 --> 25:32.156 align:middle
to be appropriate in that event.

25:33.246 --> 25:34.586 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

25:34.726 --> 25:35.446 align:middle
Chair Yellen, over here.

25:35.556 --> 25:37.086 align:middle
Hi, Peter Barnes
with Fox Business.

25:37.236 --> 25:39.276 align:middle
Could you get a little bit
more specific about the global

25:39.276 --> 25:41.386 align:middle
and economic financial
developments that continue

25:41.386 --> 25:43.316 align:middle
to pose risks to
the U.S. economy?

25:43.386 --> 25:46.176 align:middle
You did mention a strong
dollar there just a second ago

25:46.176 --> 25:48.256 align:middle
and slowing global growth.

25:48.526 --> 25:51.836 align:middle
But are you specifically
concerned about, for example,

25:51.866 --> 25:56.156 align:middle
China, the emerging
markets, and the E.U.?

25:56.326 --> 25:58.376 align:middle
Could you expand on the risks?

25:58.376 --> 25:58.666 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

25:58.806 --> 26:02.056 align:middle
So there has been,
by many forecasters,

26:02.056 --> 26:07.276 align:middle
a slight downgrading of
forecasts of global growth in--

26:07.376 --> 26:09.556 align:middle
over the coming several years.

26:09.556 --> 26:13.426 align:middle
The IMF has slightly
downgraded their forecasts,

26:13.426 --> 26:17.336 align:middle
and other international
agencies have as well.

26:17.736 --> 26:23.496 align:middle
Chinese growth hasn't
proven a great surprise.

26:23.496 --> 26:28.806 align:middle
We've anticipated that it would
slow over time, and it seems

26:28.806 --> 26:30.476 align:middle
to be slowing as well.

26:33.056 --> 26:37.316 align:middle
Japanese growth in the
fourth quarter was negative,

26:37.806 --> 26:41.196 align:middle
and that was something
of a surprise.

26:41.196 --> 26:43.526 align:middle
And with respect
to the euro area,

26:45.836 --> 26:49.806 align:middle
recent indicators suggest
perhaps slightly weaker growth.

26:49.806 --> 26:53.976 align:middle
So there's been a number
of emerging markets--

26:53.976 --> 27:00.936 align:middle
as you know, we're suffering
under the weight of declines

27:00.936 --> 27:04.626 align:middle
in oil prices that are affecting
their economic activity.

27:05.026 --> 27:09.806 align:middle
Our neighbors both to the north
and south, Canada and Mexico,

27:09.806 --> 27:15.676 align:middle
are feeling the impacts of lower
oil prices on their growth.

27:16.056 --> 27:21.986 align:middle
So our projection for global
growth, for those reasons,

27:21.986 --> 27:25.116 align:middle
is slightly lower--
not dramatically lower,

27:25.326 --> 27:29.116 align:middle
but enough lower to make some
difference to our forecast.

27:29.116 --> 27:33.826 align:middle
And, as I indicated, I think
that's part of the reason,

27:33.826 --> 27:37.666 align:middle
along with the associated
increase we have seen

27:37.826 --> 27:42.006 align:middle
in some spreads that
are involved in--

27:42.296 --> 27:44.576 align:middle
enter into corporate
borrowing rates,

27:44.916 --> 27:47.346 align:middle
and can affect investment
decisions.

27:47.616 --> 27:51.766 align:middle
It's a reason to think
that a slightly lower path

27:51.766 --> 27:54.196 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate will be appropriate

27:54.266 --> 27:56.546 align:middle
to achieve our objectives.

27:56.546 --> 28:02.366 align:middle
And so what you see here is
a virtually unchanged path

28:02.366 --> 28:08.666 align:middle
of economic projections and a
slightly more accommodative path

28:08.706 --> 28:11.126 align:middle
that most participants
are writing

28:11.126 --> 28:13.486 align:middle
down for what's necessary
to achieve that.

28:14.886 --> 28:15.876 align:middle
KATE DAVIDSON.

28:19.236 --> 28:20.196 align:middle
Thank you.

28:20.706 --> 28:22.926 align:middle
Kate Davidson from the
Wall Street Journal.

28:23.726 --> 28:26.306 align:middle
Madam Chair, you've emphasized
repeatedly that every meeting

28:26.306 --> 28:27.806 align:middle
of the FOMC is a live meeting.

28:27.886 --> 28:29.166 align:middle
You have a meeting next month.

28:29.356 --> 28:32.026 align:middle
Is it possible that you
could get enough information

28:32.026 --> 28:35.566 align:middle
between now and then to, you
know, get you comfortable

28:35.566 --> 28:37.236 align:middle
with raising rates
again in April,

28:37.236 --> 28:38.346 align:middle
and what would you need to see?

28:39.756 --> 28:40.156 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

28:40.776 --> 28:44.136 align:middle
So I will say again that every
meeting is a live meeting.

28:44.536 --> 28:47.146 align:middle
April remains a live meeting,

28:47.636 --> 28:51.486 align:middle
and we will be tracking
incoming data.

28:51.486 --> 28:53.456 align:middle
It's a slightly shorter period.

28:53.456 --> 28:56.896 align:middle
We have six weeks, but there
will be additional data

28:56.896 --> 29:02.436 align:middle
on the labor market
and on various factors

29:02.436 --> 29:04.356 align:middle
that pertain to inflation.

29:04.396 --> 29:06.866 align:middle
So that's, you know, that's--

29:07.236 --> 29:09.556 align:middle
that is certainly
a live possibility.

29:14.026 --> 29:16.136 align:middle
STEVEN MUFSON.

29:16.236 --> 29:17.116 align:middle
Two questions.

29:17.506 --> 29:20.036 align:middle
The oil-- lower oil prices.

29:20.036 --> 29:21.806 align:middle
I think a lot of
people expect it to lead

29:21.876 --> 29:23.346 align:middle
to more consumer spending.

29:23.886 --> 29:26.866 align:middle
What do you-- how do you
see and how do you explain

29:26.866 --> 29:28.896 align:middle
that that hasn't
worked out as well--

29:29.016 --> 29:30.896 align:middle
the way a lot of
people expected?

29:31.476 --> 29:35.596 align:middle
And also, if oil prices were
to pop back up to, say, $50,

29:35.596 --> 29:39.576 align:middle
not that high by some
standards, what impact would

29:39.576 --> 29:40.556 align:middle
that have on inflation?

29:40.556 --> 29:41.936 align:middle
And would you be
paying more attention

29:41.936 --> 29:43.256 align:middle
to the overall inflation rate?

29:43.256 --> 29:46.496 align:middle
Or would you then look to
the core rate to-- to--

29:47.206 --> 29:50.566 align:middle
to determine what the
Fed's policies would be?

29:50.946 --> 29:51.396 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

29:51.606 --> 29:54.506 align:middle
So let me start with the impact

29:54.506 --> 29:56.956 align:middle
of oil prices on
consumer spending.

29:58.026 --> 30:01.106 align:middle
I have to say, it's very
difficult when you look

30:01.106 --> 30:03.836 align:middle
at patterns of consumer
spending to--

30:03.886 --> 30:06.376 align:middle
there are many factors
that influence it,

30:06.736 --> 30:09.056 align:middle
and to definitively say

30:09.056 --> 30:14.236 align:middle
that lower oil prices have
not boosted consumer spending,

30:14.706 --> 30:19.126 align:middle
I'm not sure we can really
arrive at that conclusion

30:19.416 --> 30:22.316 align:middle
in any-- in any rigorous way.

30:22.786 --> 30:27.666 align:middle
The typical-- the average
household in the United States

30:27.666 --> 30:33.866 align:middle
with oil prices where they
are now is probably benefiting

30:33.866 --> 30:36.036 align:middle
around $1,000 a year.

30:36.466 --> 30:40.556 align:middle
And some very detailed
microdata that I've seen

30:40.556 --> 30:44.446 align:middle
on household spending
patterns suggest

30:44.666 --> 30:47.126 align:middle
that there may be a link,
as you would expect,

30:47.126 --> 30:53.126 align:middle
from reduced bills-- you know,
reduced amounts that people pay

30:53.446 --> 30:57.276 align:middle
at the pump to other
spending, like eating out at--

30:57.356 --> 31:00.326 align:middle
for restaurant meals
and other things.

31:00.646 --> 31:06.016 align:middle
But the aggregate data, I-- you
know, is not as strong as it--

31:06.016 --> 31:10.426 align:middle
and spending is not as strong as
it could be, given the decline.

31:10.976 --> 31:14.886 align:middle
And, of course, on the
other side, we are--

31:14.886 --> 31:17.626 align:middle
and, you know, it may be
that it will take a while,

31:17.626 --> 31:21.996 align:middle
and it's something that will
slowly strengthen over time

31:21.996 --> 31:24.096 align:middle
if oil prices stay low.

31:24.316 --> 31:28.206 align:middle
On the other side, of course,
we have seen a marked decline

31:28.206 --> 31:33.336 align:middle
in drilling activity, which has
depressed investment spending,

31:33.336 --> 31:38.246 align:middle
and, of course, very substantial
layoffs in the energy sector.

31:39.796 --> 31:43.626 align:middle
With respect to impact of
oil prices on inflation

31:43.626 --> 31:46.576 align:middle
and what would happen
if they move up,

31:47.386 --> 31:53.426 align:middle
the Committee has generally
tended to look through movements

31:53.426 --> 31:57.316 align:middle
in oil prices, whether
they were on the upside

31:57.316 --> 32:03.996 align:middle
or on the downside,
viewing it as a factor

32:04.166 --> 32:06.996 align:middle
that should have a
transitory influence.

32:07.336 --> 32:12.436 align:middle
And when I say that, what I
mean is that if oil prices move

32:12.436 --> 32:15.846 align:middle
up during the time that it's
moving up, it raises inflation.

32:16.326 --> 32:20.766 align:middle
But they don't need to move down
again to their previous levels

32:21.136 --> 32:24.576 align:middle
for that influence to
disappear, they only need

32:24.576 --> 32:26.906 align:middle
to stabilize at a higher level.

32:27.346 --> 32:30.856 align:middle
And, similarly, oil
prices have obviously moved

32:30.856 --> 32:33.656 align:middle
down a great deal
over the last year,

32:34.046 --> 32:36.966 align:middle
and we're not expecting
them to move back

32:37.086 --> 32:43.006 align:middle
to their previous levels but
to stabilize at some level.

32:43.006 --> 32:46.286 align:middle
They're obviously volatile,
but, as they stabilize,

32:46.526 --> 32:51.436 align:middle
the influence will move
out of both headline--

32:51.746 --> 32:53.576 align:middle
of headline inflation.

32:54.036 --> 32:59.496 align:middle
And that's what you see in
the forecasts of participants.

32:59.866 --> 33:03.256 align:middle
So if oil prices were to
increase to 50, I mean,

33:03.256 --> 33:08.916 align:middle
that would probably slightly
move up our expected path

33:08.956 --> 33:14.916 align:middle
for core inflation, maybe speed
how rapidly we would move back

33:14.976 --> 33:17.166 align:middle
to 2 percent, but
I wouldn't think

33:17.166 --> 33:18.996 align:middle
that that would be
something alone

33:18.996 --> 33:21.436 align:middle
that would have great
policy significance.

33:22.086 --> 33:27.146 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

33:27.146 --> 33:28.896 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

33:29.696 --> 33:33.026 align:middle
You said in-- the policy
statement in December said

33:33.196 --> 33:35.426 align:middle
that the risks to
the outlook were

33:35.426 --> 33:37.966 align:middle
"balanced," and you
raised rates.

33:38.416 --> 33:41.846 align:middle
That phrasing disappeared
then in January and March.

33:42.366 --> 33:44.316 align:middle
Could you talk a
little bit about--

33:44.356 --> 33:48.426 align:middle
but in your comments today, you
seem to be indicating that not

33:48.426 --> 33:49.676 align:middle
that much has changed.

33:50.526 --> 33:53.636 align:middle
Could you help us understand
what needs to happen to get back

33:53.706 --> 33:56.336 align:middle
to "balanced," and is that
the language we need to look

33:56.336 --> 33:58.226 align:middle
for to see the next rate hike?

33:58.226 --> 33:58.586 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:59.476 --> 34:03.836 align:middle
So let me say that,
in recent weeks,

34:04.086 --> 34:08.906 align:middle
I think the Committee
certainly thinks that risks

34:08.906 --> 34:10.906 align:middle
to the outlook have diminished.

34:11.436 --> 34:18.036 align:middle
Nevertheless, we continue to
see risks, which we highlighted.

34:19.436 --> 34:23.106 align:middle
Now, I would point out
that we decided not

34:23.156 --> 34:27.276 align:middle
to describe the balance of risks
as weighted to the downside.

34:27.736 --> 34:31.146 align:middle
So the Committee did
not reach that judgment,

34:31.636 --> 34:37.996 align:middle
and there is no collective
judgment in this statement

34:38.336 --> 34:40.876 align:middle
on whether the risks
are balanced or not.

34:40.876 --> 34:45.126 align:middle
My-- we declined to make
a collective assessment.

34:45.126 --> 34:49.126 align:middle
You know, my guess is that
some participants see them

34:49.126 --> 34:51.316 align:middle
as balanced, and some see them

34:51.316 --> 34:53.666 align:middle
as weighted somewhat
to the downside.

34:54.146 --> 34:57.446 align:middle
But I think it is important
to note a couple of things.

34:57.776 --> 35:02.086 align:middle
First of all, the U.S.
economy has been very resilient

35:02.086 --> 35:06.866 align:middle
in recent months in the face
of shocks, and we highlighted

35:06.866 --> 35:10.596 align:middle
that right at the beginning of
our statement, where we said

35:10.596 --> 35:13.606 align:middle
that "economic activity
has been expanding

35:13.606 --> 35:16.766 align:middle
at a moderate pace
despite the global economic

35:16.996 --> 35:19.576 align:middle
and financial developments
of recent months."

35:19.836 --> 35:22.216 align:middle
And that-- that is important,

35:22.286 --> 35:26.216 align:middle
that the U.S. economy
continues to do well.

35:26.456 --> 35:31.296 align:middle
Second, I would say that while
global developments do pose some

35:31.296 --> 35:35.136 align:middle
downside risks, the risks
are not all one sided.

35:35.626 --> 35:39.916 align:middle
A number of countries--
including China, the euro area,

35:39.916 --> 35:42.606 align:middle
the Bank of Japan--
have taken measures

35:42.606 --> 35:46.646 align:middle
to stimulate the economy,
so there is also upside risk

35:46.646 --> 35:48.106 align:middle
to the economic outlook.

35:48.496 --> 35:52.086 align:middle
And, in addition, oil prices
have rebounded from their lows,

35:52.426 --> 35:56.446 align:middle
and that eases concerns
about the financial condition

35:56.446 --> 35:59.886 align:middle
of some energy firms and
the stresses facing some

35:59.886 --> 36:02.066 align:middle
oil-producing economies.

36:02.306 --> 36:05.526 align:middle
And, at the same time,
low oil prices continue

36:05.526 --> 36:07.946 align:middle
to boost household
purchasing power.

36:08.166 --> 36:10.236 align:middle
So there are risks.

36:10.446 --> 36:12.136 align:middle
We're attentive to them.

36:12.436 --> 36:15.216 align:middle
We have not described them
as unbalanced to the out--

36:15.336 --> 36:18.516 align:middle
to the downside, and
they are two sided.

36:18.516 --> 36:22.046 align:middle
One can identify both upside
and downside risks there.

36:24.106 --> 36:26.346 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

36:28.126 --> 36:31.006 align:middle
Hi, Jim Puzzanghera
with the L.A. Times.

36:31.126 --> 36:33.146 align:middle
Wage growth thus far
has been disappointing.

36:33.146 --> 36:34.276 align:middle
It's been very uneven.

36:34.276 --> 36:37.706 align:middle
It was disappointing, figures
in last month's jobs report.

36:38.226 --> 36:39.176 align:middle
Why do you think that is,

36:39.176 --> 36:41.376 align:middle
and how important is
sustained wage growth

36:41.406 --> 36:43.476 align:middle
to removing your
wariness on inflation?

36:44.036 --> 36:45.146 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:45.246 --> 36:50.836 align:middle
So I must say I'm-- I do
see broad-based improvement

36:50.836 --> 36:54.496 align:middle
in the labor market, and
I'm somewhat surprised

36:54.496 --> 36:58.376 align:middle
that we're not seeing more
of a pickup in wage growth.

36:59.216 --> 37:05.596 align:middle
But at least-- and I have to
say, in anecdotal reports,

37:06.076 --> 37:09.096 align:middle
we do hear quite a
number of reports

37:09.096 --> 37:15.446 align:middle
of firms facing wage
pressures and even broad-based,

37:15.446 --> 37:19.226 align:middle
slightly faster increases
in wages--

37:19.686 --> 37:24.036 align:middle
wage increases that
they're granting.

37:24.656 --> 37:27.336 align:middle
But in the aggregate
data, one doesn't

37:27.336 --> 37:32.176 align:middle
yet see any convincing evidence
of a pickup in wage growth.

37:33.106 --> 37:37.756 align:middle
It's mainly isolated to
certain sectors and occupations.

37:38.326 --> 37:40.346 align:middle
So I do think, consistent

37:40.346 --> 37:42.716 align:middle
with the 2 percent
inflation objective,

37:42.776 --> 37:47.516 align:middle
that there is certainly scope
for further increases in wages.

37:47.976 --> 37:52.856 align:middle
The fact that we have not seen
any broad-based pickup is one

37:52.856 --> 37:56.906 align:middle
of the factors that suggests to
me that there is continued slack

37:56.906 --> 38:00.016 align:middle
in the labor market, but
I would expect wage growth

38:00.116 --> 38:00.986 align:middle
to move up some.

38:01.686 --> 38:03.806 align:middle
PATRICK GILLESPIE.

38:04.536 --> 38:06.676 align:middle
Patrick Gillespie with CNNMoney.

38:07.166 --> 38:10.046 align:middle
Chair Yellen, numerous polls
show-- by CNN and others--

38:10.046 --> 38:13.536 align:middle
show that the U.S. economy is
American voters' number one

38:13.536 --> 38:14.446 align:middle
concern right now.

38:14.716 --> 38:18.656 align:middle
There's a lot of negative
sentiment about the economy,

38:18.706 --> 38:21.716 align:middle
yet unemployment is low, job
gains have been pretty good

38:21.856 --> 38:25.326 align:middle
for the past year, and consumer
confidence has picked up.

38:25.656 --> 38:28.806 align:middle
Why do you think there is such
disparity between the progress--

38:28.806 --> 38:32.236 align:middle
between the economy and its
progress and how voters feel?

38:32.506 --> 38:35.406 align:middle
And my second question is, how
does any negative sentiment

38:35.406 --> 38:37.816 align:middle
about the economy factor
into your economic outlook

38:38.186 --> 38:40.256 align:middle
and the decisions you
make on monetary policy?

38:40.526 --> 38:40.806 align:middle
Thank you.

38:41.226 --> 38:41.866 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:41.966 --> 38:45.396 align:middle
Let me start with your
second question, if I might.

38:45.836 --> 38:50.726 align:middle
So in trying to judge the
outlook for the economy,

38:50.726 --> 38:54.586 align:middle
we do look at measures
pertaining

38:54.586 --> 38:58.476 align:middle
to consumer sentiment, and
they are in solid territory.

38:59.036 --> 39:02.426 align:middle
Household balance
sheets are much improved.

39:03.326 --> 39:08.156 align:middle
Gains in inflation-adjusted
disposable income are running

39:08.156 --> 39:09.686 align:middle
at a healthy pace.

39:09.686 --> 39:14.386 align:middle
As I mentioned, households have
benefited pretty significantly

39:14.386 --> 39:18.356 align:middle
from lower oil prices,
and measures

39:18.356 --> 39:23.936 align:middle
of consumer sentiment do
reflect-- do reflect that.

39:24.016 --> 39:27.526 align:middle
So they are not at low levels.

39:27.856 --> 39:30.476 align:middle
And, really, the
labor market, I think,

39:30.476 --> 39:32.166 align:middle
has improved a great deal.

39:32.486 --> 39:35.326 align:middle
And every demographic
group that, you know,

39:35.326 --> 39:39.846 align:middle
we track regularly
has seen improvement

39:39.846 --> 39:43.036 align:middle
in their labor market
situation--

39:43.236 --> 39:45.076 align:middle
perhaps not all equally,

39:45.076 --> 39:49.726 align:middle
but almost all demographic
groups have seen improvement.

39:49.726 --> 39:55.296 align:middle
So I-- I think it's right to
say the economy is improving,

39:55.526 --> 39:57.816 align:middle
and most groups are
seeing benefits.

39:58.126 --> 40:03.136 align:middle
That said, we know that
inequality has been rising

40:03.136 --> 40:08.406 align:middle
in the United States over many
years, not just the last several

40:08.406 --> 40:10.696 align:middle
but going back to the mid-'80s.

40:11.176 --> 40:15.136 align:middle
There has been downward
pressure on real wage groups--

40:15.416 --> 40:20.676 align:middle
on real wage gains for
groups, particularly those

40:20.676 --> 40:24.356 align:middle
that are less skilled
and educated,

40:24.576 --> 40:31.826 align:middle
and those longer-term trends
that may be associated

40:31.826 --> 40:33.176 align:middle
with a number of factors--

40:33.176 --> 40:36.336 align:middle
technological change
and globalization--

40:36.586 --> 40:42.176 align:middle
have been a concern for many,
many years, and that may be part

40:42.176 --> 40:45.316 align:middle
of what you're-- we're
seeing expressed.

40:46.596 --> 40:50.816 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

40:50.936 --> 40:51.566 align:middle
Thank you, right here.

40:52.006 --> 40:55.006 align:middle
Chair Yellen, there seems to be
some growing sentiment that even

40:55.006 --> 40:57.426 align:middle
if the direct economic
ties between the U.S.

40:57.426 --> 41:02.166 align:middle
and other economies is
still relatively modest,

41:02.746 --> 41:06.036 align:middle
that the impact through
financial markets

41:06.306 --> 41:09.306 align:middle
and exchange rates has
become more robust.

41:10.086 --> 41:14.136 align:middle
And because of that, it's
becoming more difficult

41:14.136 --> 41:19.116 align:middle
or will become more difficult
for the Fed to diverge

41:19.226 --> 41:20.826 align:middle
from other major central banks

41:20.986 --> 41:22.426 align:middle
with its benchmark
interest rate.

41:22.556 --> 41:25.706 align:middle
Would you generally agree
with that sentiment?

41:25.706 --> 41:26.796 align:middle
Why or why not?

41:27.256 --> 41:28.946 align:middle
And, very briefly,
second, if I may--

41:29.666 --> 41:34.116 align:middle
in your mind, does it undermine
in any way the standing

41:34.226 --> 41:38.286 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve as a
nonpartisan institution when one

41:38.286 --> 41:41.126 align:middle
of its sitting Governors
contributes to the campaign

41:41.376 --> 41:43.076 align:middle
of a national political figure?

41:43.446 --> 41:43.726 align:middle
Thank you.

41:44.706 --> 41:45.106 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

41:45.106 --> 41:47.736 align:middle
So let me start with
the question pertaining

41:47.736 --> 41:49.096 align:middle
to exchange rates.

41:49.596 --> 41:54.266 align:middle
We have global capital markets.

41:56.276 --> 42:01.966 align:middle
In-- in a world with highly
integrated capital markets,

42:02.436 --> 42:07.576 align:middle
monetary policy actions in
any country have effects--

42:07.576 --> 42:09.326 align:middle
spillovers to other countries.

42:09.326 --> 42:13.436 align:middle
That's true of our monetary
policy, and it's true

42:13.436 --> 42:15.836 align:middle
of other countries'
monetary policies.

42:16.266 --> 42:20.486 align:middle
In part, that shows up through
movements in exchange rates,

42:20.876 --> 42:27.636 align:middle
and those movements are a factor
that any country needs to take

42:27.636 --> 42:32.906 align:middle
into account in deciding what
is the appropriate stance

42:33.116 --> 42:34.696 align:middle
of monetary policy.

42:34.696 --> 42:41.946 align:middle
So the fact that there are these
linkages is an important factor

42:41.946 --> 42:44.076 align:middle
in designing a monetary policy.

42:44.596 --> 42:46.906 align:middle
But it does not mean
that monetary--

42:46.906 --> 42:51.926 align:middle
U.S. monetary policy is
somehow constrained in a way

42:51.926 --> 42:55.676 align:middle
that makes it impossible
for our monetary policy

42:55.736 --> 42:58.926 align:middle
to diverge from policies abroad.

42:59.196 --> 43:03.246 align:middle
There are many periods
when monetary policies

43:03.246 --> 43:07.876 align:middle
in different countries have
moved in different directions,

43:08.256 --> 43:13.466 align:middle
and, you know, the United States
has been growing more strongly

43:13.466 --> 43:16.366 align:middle
and had better success
in the labor market

43:16.436 --> 43:21.396 align:middle
than many other advanced
countries.

43:21.746 --> 43:25.116 align:middle
And, at this point, it's natural

43:25.186 --> 43:27.176 align:middle
that there should
be some divergence

43:27.556 --> 43:29.416 align:middle
in our monetary policies.

43:30.116 --> 43:33.476 align:middle
Movements in exchange
rates partly reflect that.

43:33.476 --> 43:37.716 align:middle
So I wouldn't want to endorse
the notion if, you know--

43:37.716 --> 43:42.386 align:middle
that you're suggesting, that our
policy is in some way crippled

43:42.416 --> 43:45.176 align:middle
by the fact that there
are these interlinkages.

43:45.366 --> 43:48.846 align:middle
That's simply how the global
financial system works.

43:48.846 --> 43:51.986 align:middle
We-- this is one
reason we meet--

43:52.336 --> 43:56.836 align:middle
meet frequently with other
economic policymakers

43:56.836 --> 43:59.976 align:middle
in other countries,
exchange notes

43:59.976 --> 44:04.096 align:middle
about how we see economic
developments evolve,

44:04.096 --> 44:07.426 align:middle
and try to keep one
another apprised

44:08.286 --> 44:12.436 align:middle
of economic developments
and likely policy responses.

44:13.316 --> 44:19.396 align:middle
Your second question concerned
the political contributions.

44:19.856 --> 44:23.336 align:middle
So I want to start by saying
that I've been involved

44:23.336 --> 44:25.926 align:middle
for many years in the
Federal Reserve System,

44:26.356 --> 44:33.136 align:middle
and we are a nonpartisan,
independent institution devoted

44:33.226 --> 44:37.456 align:middle
to pursuing our congressionally
mandated objectives,

44:37.806 --> 44:42.126 align:middle
and I have never
seen political views

44:42.416 --> 44:45.986 align:middle
in any way influence
the policy judgments

44:46.016 --> 44:48.806 align:middle
that are made inside
the Federal Reserve.

44:49.306 --> 44:52.166 align:middle
I want to say that emphatically.

44:53.876 --> 45:03.546 align:middle
The political activities
of Governors, participants,

45:03.936 --> 45:06.966 align:middle
government employees are
governed by the Hatch Act.

45:07.486 --> 45:09.856 align:middle
We are all subject to that,

45:10.256 --> 45:16.006 align:middle
and the Hatch Act does
allow campaign contributions

45:16.006 --> 45:18.856 align:middle
to be made.

45:19.246 --> 45:23.586 align:middle
It outlaws other forms
of partisan activity.

45:24.026 --> 45:27.486 align:middle
And I would say, within
that, so it is each--

45:27.486 --> 45:32.816 align:middle
it's up to each individual to
decide what is appropriate,

45:32.816 --> 45:34.086 align:middle
in their point of view.

45:34.416 --> 45:37.676 align:middle
But the Federal Reserve is not--

45:37.716 --> 45:40.156 align:middle
is not a partisan
political organization.

45:41.016 --> 45:43.116 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

45:44.516 --> 45:46.646 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

45:46.966 --> 45:48.596 align:middle
The policy, the projections--

45:48.636 --> 45:51.406 align:middle
the new economic projections
appear to reflect an increase

45:51.406 --> 45:53.696 align:middle
in your estimate of the
extent of labor market slack.

45:54.326 --> 45:56.176 align:middle
You talked about one
possible reason for that,

45:56.176 --> 45:58.196 align:middle
which is the softness
of wage growth.

45:58.546 --> 46:00.576 align:middle
Are there other considerations
that have led you

46:00.576 --> 46:01.926 align:middle
to increase your estimate

46:02.016 --> 46:03.876 align:middle
of how much slack there
is in the labor market?

46:04.526 --> 46:05.636 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:05.636 --> 46:08.916 align:middle
So I should point out that
I think what you're talking

46:08.916 --> 46:13.236 align:middle
about is the slight decline
in the median estimate

46:13.236 --> 46:15.556 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

46:15.556 --> 46:18.226 align:middle
Is that-- is that what
you're-- BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

46:18.546 --> 46:29.806 align:middle
That, and the slope of the
path of rate increases combined

46:29.966 --> 46:33.246 align:middle
with projections of
relatively low inflation.

46:33.246 --> 46:34.406 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:34.406 --> 46:37.936 align:middle
So I-- the path of rate
increases maybe slightly

46:37.936 --> 46:42.126 align:middle
reflects the modest
decline in estimates

46:42.126 --> 46:44.136 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

46:44.136 --> 46:46.186 align:middle
That, I would say, reflects,
for those who brought it down,

46:46.186 --> 46:48.456 align:middle
an estimate of greater
slack in the labor market.

46:48.696 --> 46:52.276 align:middle
The fed funds rate
projection is not just that.

46:52.276 --> 46:54.606 align:middle
It's also, as I mentioned,
a reflection of shifts

46:54.606 --> 46:56.306 align:middle
in other views about, for
example, the likely pace

46:56.306 --> 47:00.536 align:middle
of global growth that affects
what we need, as a policy path,

47:00.596 --> 47:02.136 align:middle
to achieve our objectives.

47:02.456 --> 47:05.216 align:middle
But the slow pace
of wage growth,

47:05.586 --> 47:11.506 align:middle
the fact that part-time
employment for economic reasons

47:11.506 --> 47:15.576 align:middle
and voluntary part-time
employment remains high--

47:16.036 --> 47:20.696 align:middle
we have seen an upward move
in labor force participation,

47:20.696 --> 47:25.576 align:middle
which is heartening and suggests
that there was scope there

47:25.576 --> 47:28.786 align:middle
for further improvement
in the labor market.

47:29.016 --> 47:34.616 align:middle
My guess is that those things
influenced individuals who wrote

47:34.856 --> 47:37.806 align:middle
down a slightly lower number

47:37.806 --> 47:40.366 align:middle
for the longer-run
unemployment rate.

47:41.886 --> 47:45.746 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

47:45.916 --> 47:48.806 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

47:48.806 --> 47:52.056 align:middle
We've heard a lot of talk today
about President Obama's pick

47:52.056 --> 47:55.576 align:middle
for the Supreme Court, but
there are a number of vacancies

47:55.576 --> 47:58.856 align:middle
on the Fed Board of Governors,
and there's a new position,

47:59.146 --> 48:00.956 align:middle
the Vice Chair[man]
for Supervision,

48:00.956 --> 48:04.086 align:middle
that the President hasn't
even nominated anyone for.

48:04.526 --> 48:06.786 align:middle
Would you like the
President to nominate someone

48:06.786 --> 48:10.376 align:middle
for that position, and what
effect are those vacancies

48:10.376 --> 48:11.316 align:middle
having on the Fed?

48:11.476 --> 48:11.806 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:12.266 --> 48:17.916 align:middle
Well, I think Congress intended
for the Federal Reserve Board

48:17.966 --> 48:25.046 align:middle
to have seven members, and that
tends to bring on board people

48:25.046 --> 48:30.126 align:middle
with a wide spectrum of views
and experience and perspectives.

48:30.416 --> 48:31.936 align:middle
I think that's valuable,

48:31.936 --> 48:35.846 align:middle
and I would like to see
the Senate move forward

48:35.846 --> 48:40.946 align:middle
and consider these nominees
so that we could operate

48:40.946 --> 48:42.346 align:middle
with a full complement.

48:42.706 --> 48:44.856 align:middle
With respect to the nomination

48:44.856 --> 48:48.576 align:middle
for Vice Chair[man] [for]
Supervision, that's a question

48:48.576 --> 48:50.896 align:middle
that I think you need to
pose to the White House.

48:50.896 --> 48:54.926 align:middle
I, you know, would say that
we are doing a very good job

48:54.926 --> 48:59.726 align:middle
on supervision, and, you
know, we are very focused on

49:00.046 --> 49:04.196 align:middle
and devote a great deal
of time to that issue.

49:04.196 --> 49:06.836 align:middle
So, really, it's--

49:06.926 --> 49:09.376 align:middle
the nomination question is
one for the White House.

49:09.986 --> 49:10.726 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

49:10.996 --> 49:14.196 align:middle
Thank you, Madam Chair.

49:14.196 --> 49:15.906 align:middle
John Heltman with
American Banker.

49:16.306 --> 49:19.606 align:middle
The President last
week expressed concern

49:19.606 --> 49:22.396 align:middle
about what he termed
"cynicism" on both sides

49:22.396 --> 49:26.476 align:middle
of the aisle concerning
progress made since the crisis

49:26.476 --> 49:28.196 align:middle
to reform the banking sector

49:28.246 --> 49:30.536 align:middle
and the financial
sector more generally.

49:31.256 --> 49:34.836 align:middle
Do you share that concern
about, kind of, public attitudes

49:34.866 --> 49:36.956 align:middle
that either not enough
has been done

49:37.376 --> 49:40.056 align:middle
or what has been done
has not been effective

49:40.056 --> 49:42.626 align:middle
in changing the regulatory
landscape in banking?

49:43.006 --> 49:44.626 align:middle
Just what are your thoughts?

49:44.806 --> 49:45.206 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:45.416 --> 49:50.536 align:middle
So I feel a great
deal has been done.

49:51.176 --> 49:56.136 align:middle
We have been working at
this for a number of years,

49:56.136 --> 50:00.296 align:middle
and I believe we have made
very substantial progress.

50:00.686 --> 50:05.306 align:middle
We have a-- much more capital,
higher-quality capital,

50:05.616 --> 50:08.206 align:middle
liquidity in the banking system.

50:08.666 --> 50:16.166 align:middle
Our supervision has-- we have
made very meaningful changes

50:16.166 --> 50:18.876 align:middle
for-- in our supervision,
for example.

50:19.286 --> 50:21.156 align:middle
The stress tests--

50:21.156 --> 50:25.166 align:middle
the stress-test methodology
that we use routinely

50:25.476 --> 50:30.676 align:middle
to evaluate the robustness of
the capital positions and plans

50:30.906 --> 50:35.816 align:middle
of the largest institutions
constitutes a quantum leap

50:35.816 --> 50:37.616 align:middle
in terms of, I think,

50:37.676 --> 50:40.946 align:middle
the quality of the
supervision we are providing,

50:41.186 --> 50:43.416 align:middle
especially of the largest firms.

50:44.556 --> 50:48.856 align:middle
So we have finished writing
most of the Dodd-Frank rules.

50:48.856 --> 50:53.676 align:middle
We are working very hard on
"too big to fail," which is

50:54.056 --> 50:57.076 align:middle
of grave concern to
the American people.

50:57.476 --> 51:00.986 align:middle
In addition to having
a financial system

51:00.986 --> 51:06.306 align:middle
that is more robust and less
likely to experience a failure,

51:06.626 --> 51:10.966 align:middle
we have also worked very
hard, continue to work

51:10.966 --> 51:14.766 align:middle
on making sure we have the
ability to resolve a firm

51:14.766 --> 51:17.356 align:middle
if it were to fail in spite--

51:17.446 --> 51:20.876 align:middle
in spite of having more
capital and liquidity.

51:21.186 --> 51:24.466 align:middle
And there, too, I think, we have
made very substantial progress.

51:24.846 --> 51:28.086 align:middle
A month or so ago, we
came out with a rule--

51:28.306 --> 51:35.676 align:middle
so-called TLAC rule-- that
would require the largest firms

51:35.976 --> 51:40.796 align:middle
to hold a substantial buffer
of long-term debt that,

51:40.796 --> 51:44.976 align:middle
in the event that they were
to fail, could be bailed

51:44.976 --> 51:51.546 align:middle
in to protect the taxpayer
from having to bear any burden

51:51.546 --> 51:55.936 align:middle
in terms of injecting
capital into the firms--

51:55.936 --> 51:59.736 align:middle
it would provide loss
absorption in that event.

52:00.066 --> 52:05.086 align:middle
And working jointly,
closely with the FDIC,

52:05.086 --> 52:08.656 align:middle
firms have made a
great deal of progress

52:08.656 --> 52:09.996 align:middle
with their living wills.

52:09.996 --> 52:11.046 align:middle
We're in the process

52:11.046 --> 52:14.486 align:middle
of evaluating the most
recent submissions.

52:14.796 --> 52:19.526 align:middle
And working with the FDIC, I
think, potential techniques

52:19.526 --> 52:22.736 align:middle
that could be used in
the event of a failure

52:22.826 --> 52:25.986 align:middle
of a significant
financial institution,

52:26.196 --> 52:28.596 align:middle
we have also made a
lot of progress there.

52:29.016 --> 52:32.686 align:middle
I think I was at the meeting
of financial regulators

52:32.686 --> 52:37.816 align:middle
with the President when he made
those comments, and I understood

52:37.816 --> 52:40.466 align:middle
that what he was trying to
tell the American people,

52:40.546 --> 52:44.236 align:middle
that they should understand
there really has been very

52:44.236 --> 52:47.416 align:middle
substantial progress, and
that's something they should be

52:47.416 --> 52:47.936 align:middle
aware of.

52:48.446 --> 52:48.816 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

52:48.846 --> 52:51.826 align:middle
Can I follow up,
just real quick?

52:52.316 --> 52:56.186 align:middle
Sorry. But do you think that,
despite all that progress,

52:56.186 --> 52:59.056 align:middle
that there-- why hasn't
that message-- why hasn't--

52:59.056 --> 53:02.166 align:middle
why haven't these improvements
made its way to the public?

53:02.606 --> 53:04.266 align:middle
And perhaps, like, do you find

53:04.306 --> 53:09.816 align:middle
that that is an obstacle either
among people that you meet,

53:10.086 --> 53:12.566 align:middle
people in the public, or the
representatives in Congress

53:12.566 --> 53:15.106 align:middle
that maybe have this pervasive
view that nothing has changed?

53:15.296 --> 53:16.316 align:middle
Is that an obstacle?

53:16.316 --> 53:17.096 align:middle
Is that a problem?

53:17.096 --> 53:18.376 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

53:18.406 --> 53:20.536 align:middle
Well, I think-- I
think it's our job

53:20.536 --> 53:25.606 align:middle
as we make these improvements
to explain what we're doing

53:25.606 --> 53:28.876 align:middle
and to try to educate the
public about what's happened,

53:29.186 --> 53:33.636 align:middle
and they may not understand
how much has changed.

53:34.466 --> 53:38.386 align:middle
It's certainly part of my own
responsibility to try to explain

53:38.386 --> 53:39.696 align:middle
that to the American people.

53:40.746 --> 53:42.126 align:middle
KAREN MRACEK.

53:42.586 --> 53:44.736 align:middle
Karen Mracek with Market
News International.

53:45.386 --> 53:51.366 align:middle
In the SEP today, the
unemployment rate forecast

53:51.366 --> 53:54.946 align:middle
for future years--
for 2017, 2018--

53:54.946 --> 53:58.486 align:middle
was marked down without changes
to inflation, and there seems

53:58.486 --> 54:03.306 align:middle
to be a growing debate about the
relationship between higher--

54:03.676 --> 54:05.546 align:middle
lower unemployment
and higher inflation.

54:06.026 --> 54:09.396 align:middle
What is your view about the
strength of this relationship,

54:09.396 --> 54:12.046 align:middle
the Phillips curve, and
how does that view weigh

54:12.046 --> 54:15.716 align:middle
in to just how much actual
inflation versus anticipation--

54:16.176 --> 54:18.076 align:middle
anticipated inflation
you'll need to see?

54:19.246 --> 54:19.516 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

54:19.516 --> 54:25.076 align:middle
Okay, so that was a complicated
question, and there--

54:26.506 --> 54:27.976 align:middle
let me start with
the Phillips curve.

54:28.566 --> 54:33.386 align:middle
So the Phillips curve posits
that there is a relationship

54:33.426 --> 54:37.576 align:middle
between the degree of slack in
the labor market and inflation,

54:38.136 --> 54:41.676 align:middle
and it is an empirical
relationship that,

54:42.026 --> 54:47.096 align:middle
while not absolutely tight, has
been a consistent relationship

54:47.096 --> 54:51.696 align:middle
over time, and I believe that
relationship still holds.

54:52.036 --> 54:56.576 align:middle
But the impact of shifts
in the unemployment rate

54:56.576 --> 55:00.016 align:middle
on inflation should
not be overstated.

55:00.176 --> 55:04.216 align:middle
The Phillips curve has become,
according to most estimates,

55:04.416 --> 55:07.576 align:middle
quite flat in the
sense that movements

55:07.576 --> 55:12.626 align:middle
in unemployment have only a
modest impact on inflation,

55:12.626 --> 55:15.876 align:middle
so we shouldn't overblow
how large that is.

55:16.306 --> 55:21.436 align:middle
In addition, the Phillips
curve theory suggests

55:21.436 --> 55:26.286 align:middle
that inflation expectations
are also an important driver

55:26.536 --> 55:29.706 align:middle
of actual wage- and
price-setting decisions

55:30.006 --> 55:31.836 align:middle
and inflation behavior,

55:32.216 --> 55:36.856 align:middle
and I believe there's also solid
empirical evidence for that.

55:37.266 --> 55:41.756 align:middle
And it's one of the reasons that
I highlighted in my statement,

55:42.026 --> 55:46.426 align:middle
and we continue to highlight
in the FOMC statement,

55:46.686 --> 55:51.836 align:middle
that we are tracking indicators
of the inflation expectations

55:52.196 --> 55:54.276 align:middle
that matter to wage
and price setting.

55:54.276 --> 55:58.176 align:middle
Now, unfortunately, we
don't have perfect measures

55:58.176 --> 55:59.006 align:middle
of these things.

55:59.296 --> 56:00.916 align:middle
We have survey measures.

56:02.366 --> 56:06.916 align:middle
We know that household measures,
even when households are asked

56:06.916 --> 56:11.506 align:middle
about longer-term inflation--
at longer-term inflation,

56:12.016 --> 56:19.446 align:middle
they tend to move in response
to salient changes in prices

56:19.676 --> 56:20.976 align:middle
that they see every day.

56:20.976 --> 56:24.346 align:middle
In particular, when
gas prices go down,

56:24.346 --> 56:27.806 align:middle
which is very noticeable
to most households,

56:28.166 --> 56:32.366 align:middle
you tend to see a view--

56:32.366 --> 56:37.776 align:middle
you tend to see responses about
long-term inflation marked down.

56:38.106 --> 56:40.886 align:middle
So that's kind of
an overresponse

56:40.936 --> 56:43.436 align:middle
to something that's transitory.

56:43.436 --> 56:51.436 align:middle
So it's difficult to get a clear
read from those survey measures.

56:51.646 --> 56:53.916 align:middle
Inflation compensation
as measured

56:53.916 --> 56:58.536 align:middle
in financial markets
also embodies a variety

56:58.536 --> 57:01.096 align:middle
of risk premia and
liquidity premia.

57:02.026 --> 57:06.256 align:middle
And so, it's also-- we monitor
those closely and discuss them

57:06.256 --> 57:09.716 align:middle
in the statement in
paragraph one, but, again,

57:10.626 --> 57:13.476 align:middle
there's not a straight
read on what's happening

57:13.506 --> 57:16.046 align:middle
to the expectations

57:16.046 --> 57:17.996 align:middle
that influence wage
and price setting.

57:18.326 --> 57:25.086 align:middle
But this model continues to at
least influence my own thinking,

57:25.166 --> 57:29.616 align:middle
and it certainly is a factor
that I and at least some

57:29.616 --> 57:32.046 align:middle
of my colleagues
are incorporating

57:32.046 --> 57:33.356 align:middle
in these projections.

57:33.876 --> 57:35.176 align:middle
ERIK SCHATZKER.

57:38.036 --> 57:40.316 align:middle
Erik Schatzker, Bloomberg
Television.

57:40.436 --> 57:41.326 align:middle
Madam Chair, thank you.

57:41.816 --> 57:46.106 align:middle
Notwithstanding what the dots
tell us about rate expectations,

57:46.416 --> 57:50.286 align:middle
has there been any discussion
among members of the Committee

57:50.626 --> 57:53.226 align:middle
about the potential need
for further stimulus?

57:53.776 --> 57:56.446 align:middle
And even if there hasn't
been such a discussion yet,

57:57.936 --> 58:00.146 align:middle
could you share with us
what you have learned

58:00.146 --> 58:02.606 align:middle
from the reevaluation of
negative interest rates,

58:03.026 --> 58:05.266 align:middle
whether you consider negative
interest rates effective,

58:05.916 --> 58:08.096 align:middle
how effective relative
to quantitative easing,

58:09.026 --> 58:11.556 align:middle
and whether the Committee would
hypothetically use them instead

58:11.556 --> 58:14.526 align:middle
of or in conjunction with
quantitative easing in the event

58:14.526 --> 58:16.826 align:middle
that the economy should
warrant further stimulus?

58:16.826 --> 58:17.116 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

58:17.576 --> 58:19.926 align:middle
Okay. So what I would
like to make clear is

58:19.926 --> 58:22.436 align:middle
that this is not
actively a subject

58:22.666 --> 58:25.086 align:middle
that we are considering
or discussing.

58:26.036 --> 58:30.666 align:middle
The Committee continues to
feel that we are on a course

58:31.086 --> 58:35.016 align:middle
where the economy is improving
and inflation is moving back up.

58:35.606 --> 58:42.366 align:middle
And, as I indicated, if events
continue to unfold in that way,

58:42.366 --> 58:46.766 align:middle
we are likely to gradually
raise rates over time.

58:46.996 --> 58:49.516 align:middle
Again, that's not
fixed in stone.

58:49.516 --> 58:52.996 align:middle
We'll watch how the
economy behaves.

58:52.996 --> 58:58.306 align:middle
We're prepared to respond if
things transpire differently,

58:58.666 --> 59:02.626 align:middle
but we are not spending
time actively debating

59:02.626 --> 59:09.106 align:middle
and considering things we could
do for additional accommodation

59:09.426 --> 59:13.416 align:middle
and certainly not actively
considering negative rates.

59:13.776 --> 59:17.486 align:middle
We are looking at the
experience in other countries,

59:17.486 --> 59:23.276 align:middle
and I guess I would judge they
seem to have mixed effects,

59:23.706 --> 59:26.116 align:middle
you know, some positive
and some negative things.

59:26.116 --> 59:30.856 align:middle
But, look, if we found ourselves
in the unlikely situation

59:30.856 --> 59:33.756 align:middle
where we needed to
add accommodation,

59:33.916 --> 59:35.576 align:middle
we have a range of tools.

59:35.926 --> 59:40.146 align:middle
And we know from the
things we did in the past

59:40.466 --> 59:45.596 align:middle
that we have a number of options
with respect to the maturity,

59:45.896 --> 59:51.106 align:middle
for example, of our portfolio,
with respect to asset purchases

59:51.396 --> 59:55.136 align:middle
or forward guidance that
remain available to us,

59:55.536 --> 59:59.506 align:middle
that are tools we could turn
to in the unlikely event

59:59.506 --> 01:00:01.646 align:middle
that we need to add
accommodation.

01:00:01.646 --> 01:00:03.886 align:middle
So negative rates
is not something

01:00:03.886 --> 01:00:05.386 align:middle
that we're actively considering.

